{"id":"typical-income","title":"Typical income","badge":"½","kind":"choropleth","time":{"years":[1990,2000,2010,2020],"default":2020,"label":"Decade"},"about":"What the typical person actually lives on: the median income (or consumption) per person per day from household surveys. Half the population lives on less, half on more. Compare it with GDP per person to see how much of the economy shows up in household budgets: \"Median vs GDP per person\" is the median times 365 as a share of GDP per capita. GDP also counts company profits, government spending and investment, and surveys miss most top incomes, so in most countries it is well below 100%; in the poorest it can come close, because surveys count home-grown food and other subsistence production that GDP largely misses. \"Median vs mean\" shows inequality among households: the lower it is, the more the average is pulled up by the top. Each decade uses the survey closest to it; the survey year is shown in brackets. Rich countries mostly survey income, many poorer ones consumption, so compare with care. All amounts in 2017 international dollars per day (purchasing-power adjusted).","source":{"name":"World Bank Poverty and Inequality Platform via Our World in Data; World Bank WDI (GDP, 2017 PPP)","url":"https://pip.worldbank.org"},"fields":[{"key":"median","label":"Median","unit":"$/day","format":"perday","primary":true,"yearKey":"survey","scale":{"type":"log","palette":"spectrum","bins":[2,3,5,8,12,20,30,50]}},{"key":"mean","label":"Mean","unit":"$/day","format":"perday","yearKey":"survey","scale":{"type":"log","palette":"spectrum","bins":[2,3,5,8,12,20,30,50]}},{"key":"p10","label":"Poorest 10% below","unit":"$/day","format":"perday","scale":{"type":"log","palette":"spectrum","bins":[1,1.5,2.5,4,6,10,15,25]}},{"key":"p90","label":"Richest 10% above","unit":"$/day","format":"perday","scale":{"type":"log","palette":"spectrum","bins":[5,8,12,20,30,50,75,110]}},{"key":"median_vs_gdp","label":"Median vs GDP per person"